2.bug_scenario:
When running Lighthouse against a script like

  switch(true){case/^hello!/.test("hello!"):document.write("///<any content>");}

Lighthouse’s “Minify JavaScript” audit (implemented in minification-estimator.js) flags the file as unminified even though it is minified. The root cause is that hasPunctuatorBefore() doesn’t recognize “case” as a punctuator before a RegExp literal, so the “/” after “case” is treated as division, the next “/” is seen as comment start, and everything after is stripped — leading Lighthouse to think it’s huge whitespace and thus unminified. The screenshot shows Lighthouse reporting 14 KiB potential savings on a truly minified script.js.
